  <ul><li><a class="wiki_link" href="/web/20141103174753/http://bartonhistory.wikispaces.com/Living+connections#David%20Barton">DBHB</a>'s great x3 grandfather.</li><li>Born 16 February 1803, son of <a class="wiki_link" href="/web/20141103174753/http://bartonhistory.wikispaces.com/Charles+Elliott+%281776-1856%29">Charles Elliott (1776-1856)</a> and <a class="wiki_link" href="/web/20141103174753/http://bartonhistory.wikispaces.com/Alicia+Boileau+%281779-1851%29">Alicia Boileau (1779-1851)</a>.</li><li>Contemporary of <a class="wiki_link_ext" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20141103174753/http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Stephenson" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Robert Stevenson (1803-1859)</a> and <a class="wiki_link_ext" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20141103174753/http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_Disraeli" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Benjamin Disraeli (1804-1881)</a>.</li><li>Travelled widely, at the expense of family life, and published several travel journals (below).</li><li>Married, in 1831, <a class="wiki_link" href="/web/20141103174753/http://bartonhistory.wikispaces.com/Emily+Dougan+%281806-1877%29">Emily Dougan (1806-1877)</a>.</li><li>Father of <a class="wiki_link" href="/web/20141103174753/http://bartonhistory.wikispaces.com/Emily+Elliott+%281839-1924%29">Emily Elliott (1839-1924)</a>.</li><li>In 1842, presented <a class="wiki_link" href="/web/20141103174753/http://bartonhistory.wikispaces.com/Baptismal+water+from+the+Jordan">water from the river Jordan</a> to <a class="wiki_link_ext" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20141103174753/http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria_of_the_United_Kingdom" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Queen Victoria</a> for the baptism of her son <a class="wiki_link" href="/web/20141103174753/http://bartonhistory.wikispaces.com/Famous+connections#Edward%20VII">Edward VII</a>.</li><li>Died 1 July 1875, aged 72, in Geneva. Buried at <a class="wiki_link" href="/web/20141103174753/http://bartonhistory.wikispaces.com/St.+Mary%27s+Church%2C+Tattingstone">St. Mary's Church, Tattingstone</a>.</li></ul><br/>
